WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.440
Good acting often requires years of training,&nbsp;
a ton of effort, and a little luck. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.040
though, no amount of any of those things can help&nbsp;
otherwise talented actors affect an accent that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.400
simply beyond their abilities. These are the&nbsp;
on-screen accents that were absolutely horrendous.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.200
Just because you once starred in&nbsp;
an Austin Powers movie doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.920
mean you can actually pull off a decent&nbsp;
British accent. Just ask Heather Graham.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.920
Perhaps the worst part of Graham's atrocious&nbsp;
accent in the 2001 Ripper flick From Hell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.520
is that she just can't seem to&nbsp;
decide which one to go with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.040
The United Kingdom is home to&nbsp;
dozens of different accents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.240
dialects — unfortunately, it seems that&nbsp;
Graham is unable to pull off any of them.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.160
"Well, you'd better throw us in jail, then, 'cos&nbsp;
we've no money for food and no money for a doss."

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.040
During this movie, Graham jumps from&nbsp;
cockney to a kind of Irish to something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.840
altogether unintelligible, proving that the&nbsp;
actress may just have benefited from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.720
more thorough accent coaching. In fact,&nbsp;
sometimes it seems like she's barely even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.680
trying at all — and simply sounds more like&nbsp;
a Hollywood actress than a Whitechapel local.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.120
"I wish I could show you the little village&nbsp;
where I was born. It's so lovely there."

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.560
Not even a spoonful of sugar can make Dick van&nbsp;
Dyke's attempt at Cockney English more palatable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.080
In fact, he himself once called his work&nbsp;
in the 1964 classic Mary Poppins, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.160
"the most atrocious cockney&nbsp;
accent in the history of cinema."

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.840
"What did I tell ya? There's&nbsp;
a whole world at your feet."

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.800
At the time, however, van Dyke didn't&nbsp;
know how far off he really was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.800
As the actor later told The Guardian:

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.160
"Someone should have told me I&nbsp;
needed to work on my Cockney accent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.920
Nearly everyone in the Mary Poppins cast was a&nbsp;
Brit, but no one said anything. [...] Years later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.360
I asked Julie [Andrews]: 'Why didn't you tell me?'&nbsp;
She said it was because I was working so hard."

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.120
Still, van Dyke continues to keep a&nbsp;
positive attitude about the whole thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.440
despite actual Brits apparently never&nbsp;
letting him forget it. He explained:

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.960
"People in the U.K. love&nbsp;
to rib me about my accent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.120
I will never live it down. They ask what&nbsp;
part of England I was meant to be from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.920
and I say it was a little shire in the north&nbsp;
where most of the people were from Ohio."

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.160
"Now, I always say there's&nbsp;
nothing like a good joke."

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.000
What happens when a French-American&nbsp;
actor plays an immortal Scottish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.560
swordsman? Well, something like this:

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.160
"This cannot be. It's the Devil's work."

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.360
Christopher Lambert's fake Scottish&nbsp;
accent in 1986's Highlander is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.040
undoubtedly one of the worst&nbsp;
to ever hit the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.040
Most of the time, Lambert barely sounds&nbsp;
Scottish at all, and the movie isn't helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.000
by the fact that actual Scottish actor&nbsp;
Sean Connery plays an Egyptian/Spaniard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.600
who sounds neither Egyptian nor Spanish. He&nbsp;
does, however, sound a lot like Sean Connery.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.480
"I am Juan Sánchez Villalobos Ramírez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.000
Chief metallurgist to King Charles V&nbsp;
of Spain. And I'm at your service."

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.080
But hey, who needs accents when&nbsp;
you have the Macleod Longsword?

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.600
Having cemented his reputation as a major&nbsp;
blockbuster movie star, in the early '90s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.320
Tom Cruise decided to prove that he could&nbsp;
really act. So, not long after earning an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.360
Oscar nomination for Born on the Fourth&nbsp;
of July, he signed up for Far and Away.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.400
Co-written and directed by Ron Howard and&nbsp;
co-starring Cruise's wife Nicole Kidman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.680
Far and Away tells the epic story of a young Irish&nbsp;
immigrant couple who arrive in the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.160
in the late 19th century, searching for a&nbsp;
better life. But in his efforts to play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:41.360
representative of the 1800s Irish diaspora, he&nbsp;
missed one major element: a decent Irish accent.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.840
"I'm not going to a distant world. I'm of&nbsp;
Ireland, and I'll stay in Ireland 'til I die."

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.640
In 2021, Irish Central called Cruise's voice&nbsp;
work, quote, "truly appalling" and added that:

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.040
"[It] sounds like how a Hollywood film&nbsp;
executive imagines Irish people talk."

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.680
Leonardo DiCaprio is one of the&nbsp;
best actors of his generation — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.160
in 2007 he earned his third Academy&nbsp;
Award nomination for Blood Diamond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:10.800
An exposé of the inhumane, brutal,&nbsp;
and deadly diamond trade in Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.440
the film takes place during the Sierra Leone Civil&nbsp;
War in the 1990s. DiCaprio plays Danny Archer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.440
a diamond smuggler turned activist who hails&nbsp;
from Zimbabwe, but spent years in South Africa.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.560
Both of those countries were colonized and long&nbsp;
occupied by European nations; namely the U.K.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.120
and the Netherlands. But DiCaprio's accent in&nbsp;
the movie doesn't sound much like a typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.360
South African accent, nor does it seem&nbsp;
English or Dutch-based either. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.920
DiCaprio's character sounds more like he might&nbsp;
be from another part of the world entirely.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:45.600
"You Americans, you Americans love&nbsp;
to talk about your feelings, huh?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.240
What does that mean, what does that mean?"

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.120
Trevor Noah, a native of South Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.920
even called out DiCaprio's voice performance&nbsp;
on a 2019 episode of The Daily Show:

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.800
"We've gotta get back to&nbsp;
those bloody get the diamonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.600
get the bloody diamonds. What are you,&nbsp;
a drunk Australian? What are you doing?"

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.600
Martin Scorsese's crime thriller The Departed is&nbsp;
an epic tale with a hefty and confident state of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:16.000
place — that place being Boston. Scorsese loaded&nbsp;
the cast with actors well-known for their Beantown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.680
roots, including Mark Wahlberg and Matt Damon, but&nbsp;
he also brought in the legendary Jack Nicholson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.800
who portrays Irish-American&nbsp;
gang boss Frank Costello.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.520
Maybe Scorsese thought that Wahlberg and&nbsp;
Damon's natural accents were enough for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.480
very Bostonian movie, or that their speech&nbsp;
patterns would simply rub off on Nicholson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.080
Or maybe he just thought that Nicholson&nbsp;
would do his job as one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.800
acclaimed actors of all time and give&nbsp;
that Massachusetts brogue a try. Alas…

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.160
“Heavy lies the crown… sorta thing.”

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.800
To be fair, sometimes Nicholson&nbsp;
does provide a serviceable accent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.160
dropping an "r" here and there like a true&nbsp;
native Boston man. But then, on occasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.501
he'll give a line reading in a genuinely&nbsp;
comical, hugely over-the-top Irish accent.

00:06:03.501 --> 00:06:07.520
"Oh, I love the dear silver&nbsp;
that shines in your hair."

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.720
Justin Timberlake is an entertainer at&nbsp;
heart. His career began in childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.200
when he was a member of the always singing,&nbsp;
dancing, and smiling Mickey Mouse Club — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.480
later found mega-fame as a member of the boy&nbsp;
band *NSYNC. Timberlake brought that same kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.960
of intense energy to some of his early film&nbsp;
acting roles, particularly that of arrogant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.560
French Canadian hockey player Jacques Grandé&nbsp;
in the Mike Myers' 2008 comedy The Love Guru.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.200
Timberlake goes as big, broad, and wacky&nbsp;
as he possibly could have in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.720
so naturally he opts for a&nbsp;
truly cartoonish French accent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.160
reminiscent more of Pepe Le Pew&nbsp;
than an actual French-speaker.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.720
"Stay away from my girlfriend! Wooooo!"

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.160
The real problem here, however, is&nbsp;
that Jacques isn't French — he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:56.400
French Canadian, which is supposed to&nbsp;
be a distinctive dialect in of itself.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:03.360
Robin Hood is a distinctively English folk hero.&nbsp;
A rogue ex-aristocrat in the English system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.200
he steals from the rich and gives to the&nbsp;
poor, operating with his band of Merry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:11.520
Men out of a home base in Sherwood&nbsp;
Forest, near the city of Nottingham.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:17.200
The tale of Robin Hood has been put to film many&nbsp;
times, including a take in 2010 that cost nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.320
$200 million. Producers likely wanted to make&nbsp;
sure they got a good return on their investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.760
and preferred a bankable star in the title&nbsp;
role — someone like Russell Crowe, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.600
Perhaps it just didn't matter, then, that Crowe&nbsp;
does not, will not, and seemingly cannot speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.400
with an East Midlands accent, the regional&nbsp;
English dialect used around Nottingham.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:48.160
"Every Englishman's home is his castle. What&nbsp;
we would ask, your majesty, is liberty."

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.440
Upon Robin Hood's release, critics quickly&nbsp;
called out Crowe's accent; the actor even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:58.720
abruptly left an interview with a BBC Radio host&nbsp;
who said he sounded more Irish than English.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.520
Eight years later, actor Con O'Neill brought&nbsp;
up Crowe's Robin Hood accent on Twitter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.880
Crowe replied that his accent&nbsp;
was supposed to be that of:

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:12.720
"A child born in Barnsley, on his father’s death,&nbsp;
taken to France at age 6, travels across Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.520
to the Middle East on foot, fights in the&nbsp;
third crusade for Richard I, alongside men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:23.040
from all parts of Britain, Ireland and France &amp;&nbsp;
finally returns to England a man in his 40’s."

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:29.840
In the 1997 action movie Con Air, Nicolas Cage&nbsp;
plays a recently paroled convict and former U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.200
Army Ranger who gets stuck on a prisoner transport&nbsp;
plane after it's hijacked by the other prisoners.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.400
His character, Cameron Poe, is said to be&nbsp;
from Alabama, so Cage affects a slow, languid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.680
drawl — and seems to be aiming for something&nbsp;
close to Forrest Gump by way of Foghorn Leghorn.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.720
“Nice jet. You can take Cindino and&nbsp;
leave the rest to rot, for all I care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.720
You fire that weapon, 20 pissed&nbsp;
off prisoners are gonna hear it."

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.600
This is an especially strange choice for&nbsp;
Cage, a notoriously quirky man who speaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.240
with a twinge of the South in real life — even&nbsp;
though he was born in Burbank, California.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:08.240
Sam Worthington starred in the&nbsp;
highest-grossing film ever released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.240
and yet he probably could still walk down&nbsp;
the street unaccosted — because the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.800
in Avatar in which his face was actually&nbsp;
on screen were kept to a bare minimum.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.240
The Australian actor's time as Jake Sully in the&nbsp;
movie is mostly spent as a combination of CGI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.160
and voice acting, as his character&nbsp;
inhabits a lab-grown avatar meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.880
to look like the inhabitant of an alien planet.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.920
Unfortunately, this means that the audience&nbsp;
is basically forced to focus on Jake's voice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:37.920
Naturally, then, many have noticed that&nbsp;
Worthington's attempt at sounding like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.840
a tough American Marine veteran&nbsp;
actually sounds like anything but.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:42.960
"I needed their help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:50.640
and they needed mine. But to ever face them again,&nbsp;
I was gonna have to take it to a whole new level."

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:55.120
His prominent Australian accent&nbsp;
leaks through often during the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:00.160
despite undergoing extensive work with a dialect&nbsp;
coach before filming began. But maybe there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.720
only so much you can work with — after all,&nbsp;
as director James Cameron told Total Film:

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.280
"His accent was thicker than Crocodile Dundee."

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.520
In a crowded cast of terrific&nbsp;
actors at the top of their game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.240
Don Cheadle somehow managed to pull off one&nbsp;
of the most memorable performances in the 2001&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:22.080
remake of Ocean's Eleven. He played Basher Tarr,&nbsp;
the member of Danny Ocean's heist crew who is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:26.960
expert in explosives — and who is supposed to&nbsp;
come from a British, working-class background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:32.000
Cheadle was born in the American Midwest, however,&nbsp;
so he worked hard with an on-set speech coach in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.440
order to perfect his character's Cockney accent.&nbsp;
Although, "perfect" might not be the right word.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:41.360
"Alright chaps — hang onto your knickers."

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:45.280
Once the movie was released, however, it&nbsp;
appears that Cheadle's accent provoked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:49.120
few strong reactions. The actor later&nbsp;
said that, on a visit to London:

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.760
"Someone would come up and say&nbsp;
'love that character, mate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.560
it's great.' And the next street&nbsp;
someone would come up and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.560 --> 00:11:00.160
'don't ever do that again!' Someone did a u-turn&nbsp;
to come drive by me and cuss me out for the part."

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:02.800
Cheadle is so over the criticism of his accent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.480
in fact, that he has even taken to supporting a&nbsp;
fan theory that explains why it sounds so off:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:12.400
that Basher is really an American&nbsp;
pretending to be a Cockney Brit.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.320
"Oh, leave it out."

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:20.960
Oliver Stone's Natural Born Killers is an&nbsp;
eye-popping, style-hopping extravaganza.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:25.840
The movie follows spree killers Mickey&nbsp;
and Mallory Knox, whose criminal fame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.880
is spurred on by Wayne Gale, the host of&nbsp;
a trashy TV show called American Maniacs.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:37.040
Robert Downey Jr. is both loathsome and bombastic&nbsp;
as the exploitative and ratings-hungry Gale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:41.120
whom Stone later explained is based&nbsp;
on a combination of Geraldo Rivera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:46.480
and Australian tabloid journalist Steve&nbsp;
Dunleavy. It's actually the latter's Australian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:51.040
accent that Downey is trying to imitate&nbsp;
here, but he doesn't quite get it right.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.640
"Do you think those nitwits out there&nbsp;
in zombieland remember anything?"

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:00.560
Instead, Downey's accent is some bizarre&nbsp;
combination of South African, a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:06.160
different British dialects, some New Zealand vocal&nbsp;
features, and perhaps just a hint of Australian.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:12.320
In 2005, Oscar-winning filmmaker Cameron&nbsp;
Crowe received the worst reviews of his career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:17.040
with his rom-com flick Elizabethtown.&nbsp;
Starring English actor Orlando Bloom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.520
it appears that Elizabethtown doesn't really&nbsp;
know what kind of movie it wants to be;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.760
it's a romantic comedy, but it's also&nbsp;
about a guy mourning his father's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:30.960
as well as a professional redemption story, and&nbsp;
a movie about a man returning to his hometown.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.080
Similarly, Bloom is all over&nbsp;
the place with his accent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.240
despite some extensive accent&nbsp;
training. At the time, the actor said:

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.640
"It wasn't easy to master an American accent. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.920
had a lot of help from a dialect&nbsp;
coach. But I did all right. No?"

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:49.520
"A billion dollars! That's a lot of million."

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.160
Bloom's character is supposed to be a Kentucky&nbsp;
native, but the actor is never able to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:59.520
commit to a specific Southern accent. The way he&nbsp;
delivers the script's dialogue in a wishy-washy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.280
non-specific American way is a little&nbsp;
reminiscent of that of his co-star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.680
Alec Baldwin… but, for some reason,&nbsp;
it also sounds kinda Scottish, too.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:08.800
"How's that going?"

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:09.760
"Sort of a wait and see."

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:10.820
"Oh yeah?"

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:12.800
"But then I waited and I saw."

